/-!
# Differentiable moment persuasion: Uniqueness and the Bayes-plausible selector

The moment-persuasion problem admits a unique optimal joint `pi*` generated by a **measurable
Bayes-plausible selector** `χ : Ω → X`: Prior-a.e. state `ω` is assigned a single active point
`χ ω` in the canonical active set, and each cell `χ⁻¹({x})` carries posterior moment `x`.

This conclusion carries substantial hypotheses beyond differentiability of the value, all visible
in `unique_optimal_joint_bayesPlausible`:

* the moment coordinates are Lipschitz (`MomentSetup.IsCoordLipschitz`);
* the value `v` is Lipschitz, `C¹`, measurable, bounded, and upper semicontinuous, and the composed
  value `μ ↦ v(E_μ[m])` is measurable and upper semicontinuous;
* the posterior-check (convex roof) of any Lipschitz price is Lipschitz on `X` (`h_pcheck_lip`);
* the prior is an open-positive measure (`IsOpenPosMeasure`);
* the moment image is dense in `X` (`s.X ⊆ closure (range m)`);
* the genericity and absolute-continuity conditions bundled in `MomentSetup.IsDifferentiable`.

It also relies on the `MomentSetup` feasibility/surjectivity field `moment_surjOn_X`: Every point
of `X` is achievable as a posterior moment, so `X` is the closed convex hull of the moment image
rather than an arbitrary compact convex superset.

In the classical statement (Dworczak–Kolotilin 2024, Theorem 7), where `Ω = X` is a real interval,
the cells `χ⁻¹({x})` are convex — the optimum is *convex-partitional*.  That convexity is not
stated here: The ambient hypotheses on `Ω` are only topological / measurable / pseudometric, so
subsets of `Ω` have no convexity to assert.  What is formalized is the measurable selector together
with the Bayes-plausibility identity; the convex-partition strengthening (vector-space structure on
`Ω` plus a `cells_convex` field) is deferred.

## Main definitions

* `MomentSetup.IsDifferentiable` — standing differentiability and genericity mixin on a
  `MomentSetup` and value function.
* `BayesPlausibleSelector` — measurable cell-assignment `χ : Ω → X` into an active set `S`
  satisfying the Bayes-plausibility identity.

## Main statements

* `unique_optimal_joint_bayesPlausible` — under `MomentSetup.IsDifferentiable`, the optimal joint
  is unique and is generated by a Bayes-plausible selector.

## Notes

The Lean-reserved identifier `π` (matrix multiplication notation) is avoided throughout: Joints are
named `pi`, and `λ` references are spelled `lambdaref`.

## References

* Dworczak, Piotr, and Anton Kolotilin. 2024. “The Persuasion Duality.” *Theoretical Economics* 19
  (4): 1701–55. [https://doi.org/10.3982/te5900](https://doi.org/10.3982/te5900). Theorem 7.

## Tags

persuasion, moment persuasion, differentiable, uniqueness, Bayes-plausible selector
-/

@[expose] public section

